ENGLISH 21

&


\noindent

The actual shiftrule is in TC\_WhShift2; this rule has so many subrules that it 
needed a separate file. 
\begin{description}
\item[TNoWhShift] to let structures where shifting does not apply pass
\item[TNoWhInfShift] to let infinitives with a wh-subject pass
\item[TExtrapWhShift] for shifting out of an extraposed subordinate clause
\item[TComplWhShift] for shifting out of an embedded subordinate clause
\item[TStrandedWhShift] for shifting of the object of some prepp (locargrel, 
dirargrel, prepobjrel)
\end{description}

No rules have been written yet for shift of the object of the prepobj in an 
NP(VAR) (Who did you take advantage of (idiom); Who did you take a picture of).
Since the order of shift and substitution is undefined there, no solution for 
this case is apparent.\\
Also to be written is a rule for shift of the object of a THANP: Who are you 
bigger than? (The Dutch version needs no shift: Groter dan wie ben jij?)

\newpage
\begin{verbatim}

%
% TRANSFORMATION TNoWhShift
< m1: CLAUSE{CLAUSErec1}
            [ I1:mu1, 
              predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}[I2:mu2], 
              I3:mu3 
            ]
>

< m : CLAUSE{CLAUSErec1}
            [ I1:mu1, 
              predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}[I2:mu2], 
              I3:mu3 
            ]
>


   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       I1: NOT EXIST (mu1, [I11:../T1]) OR
           ( EXIST (mu1, [I11:shiftrel/T1]) AND
             EXIST (mu1, [I13:../VERB{VERBrec1}[mu5]]) )
       I2: NOT EXIST (mu2, [I11:../T1]) AND
           NOT EXIST (mu2, [complrel/I5::SENTENCE{SENTENCErec1}[I4:mu4]])
       I3: NOT EXIST (mu3, [I11:../T1])  AND
           NOT EXIST (mu3, [extraposrel/I5::SENTENCE{SENTENCErec1}[I4:mu4]])
       I4: EXIST (mu4, [I11:shiftrel/T1]) OR 
           ( EXIST (mu4, [I12:subjrel/T2]) AND
             SENTENCErec1.finiteness = infinite )
         I11: ( T1.CAT IN [PREPP, VARPREPP, PREPPVAR, ADJP, 
                           ADVP, ADVPVAR, NPVAR] 
                WITH T1.REC.mood IN [wh, relativexpmood, topicmood] ) 
              OR
              ( T1.CAT IN [CNVAR] )
         I12: ( T2.CAT IN [NPVAR] WITH T2.REC.mood IN [wh, relativexpmood])
              OR 
              ( T2.CAT IN [CNVAR] )
         I13: VERBrec1.subc = modalverb
       I5: SENTENCErec1.mood = declarative
      >

      COMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: @
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: @
         >
&

\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] TNoWhShift
\item[* relevant examples:] x1 did leave, x1 said that he had left, to leave 
now (is a crime), x1 does not know who has left, etc.
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] clauseTOsentence
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) TC\_WhShift
\item[* date of creation:] 10-JUN-1988 
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:] Dutch Twhshift0
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: 03/10/89\ \ \ \ \ author: margreet sanders\\
Added VARPREPP and added topicmood.
\item date: 04/10/89\ \ \ \ \ author: margreet sanders\\
allowed shiftrel/.. in case there is a modal
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] to let clauses in which there is nothing to shift pass this 
transformation class
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals) NPs are still 
not mentioned in the present rule, i.e.\ a shifted wh-NP may pass through the 
NoShift rule. This is needed to prevent many ambiguous paths in analysis of 
sentences like `What did you think Pete saw', where the order of shift and 
substitution interacts. This is a problem for predicative NPs (What do you 
think he is). No solution is apparent: blocking of (de)substitution of shifted 
NPs in declarative subsentences (i.e.\ not turning an NP into an NPVAR during 
the rule cycle of the embedded clause) is not possible, since it is not known 
that the clause has been/will be a subsentence.
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:]
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
