\begin{description}
\item[* name:] TComplWhshift
\item[* relevant examples:] \mbox{}\\
1. you did see [whom him hit] yesterday $\rightarrow$ whom 
you did see [him hit] yesterday\\
2. you did see [whom escape] $\rightarrow$ whom you did see [escape]\\
$^{*}$who seemed [to be ill] $\leftarrow$ seemed [who to be ill]
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] CLAUSEtoSENTENCE
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) TC\_WhShift
\item[* date of creation:] 12-AUG-1988 
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:] TExtrapShift
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] to shift wh-elemements from an embedded non-extraposed 
sentence into the higher sentence
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals) especially for 
analysis of closedtoinfs, conditions on the CLAUSE are formulated, to prevent 
false paths starting here, in which the shifted element would be placed in the 
subject position of the embedded sentence right away, without having functioned 
as the subject of the main clause first (and hence being blocked by the 
ObjectOKrules).
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:] one subrule for 
every case 
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:]
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
